# Reflection pass

Reasoning and record formats: `docs/discipline/reflection-loop.md`. Routing rules:
`notes/reflect-autonomy-heuristics.md`. Run this in the main session at a stopping point;
a worker never runs a pass.

## 1. Read what is new — once

```bash
bin/journal --show-new
bin/journal --summary
```

`--show-new` prints the unreviewed rows of `logs/friction.jsonl`, `logs/failures.jsonl`,
`logs/feedback.jsonl` and the new lines of `notes/workflow-journal.md`, and **records
exactly what it printed**. Do not run it again in this pass: a row logged during the pass
must stay new for the next one. Read `priority: now` friction rows first. If nothing is new,
say so and stop.

Also read `logs/nudges.jsonl`: a nudge that fires often, especially one usually followed by
the same action anyway, is a cluster like any other. Narrow it if its pattern is too broad
(AUTO: a repair), or propose removing it (ASK: it removes a mechanism). And read
`git log --oneline --grep='reflect auto'` for auto-applied fixes since the last pass.
{{#if modules.usage}}

Optional: `bin/usage-report --since <date of the last pass>` (the date in the journal's last
`<!-- reviewed … -->` marker) shows how the model work split between models, and between
the main thread and subagents, since then. A shift worth acting on (say, the main thread
now writing most of the output) is a cluster like any other; a model change it suggests is
ASK-class.

Every pass: read the report's `largest main-thread context` line against the
auto-compaction limit ({{#if harness.is_claude}}`autoCompactWindow` in `.claude/settings.json`{{else}}`model_auto_compact_token_limit` in `.codex/config.toml`{{/if}}; {{harness.auto_compact_tokens}} at
init, a starting value). When sessions keep reaching the limit, or it has not been revisited
in several weeks, a lower limit is a candidate ASK fix: give the measured peak, what a
lower limit saves (cheaper turns, a more focused main thread) and what it risks (details not
written to disk are lost at each compaction). Do not propose it again after {{owner}}
declined it unless the numbers changed.
{{/if}}

## 2. Cluster

Group the rows by theme: the same failure signature, the same kind of friction, the same
correction from {{owner}}. Count each cluster. A single row is a cluster of one — note it,
but a rule from one incident is ASK-class at best.

An intake question {{owner}} has answered the same way twice (in the work folders' recorded
intake answers, or the conversation) is a candidate standing procedure: propose the line for
`notes/procedures.md`. Always ASK: only {{owner}} edits that file.
{{#if modules.questions}}

Read the `override:` lines in the work folders' READMEs: tasks {{owner}} ran although the
tie-auditor did not pass them. Two overrides with the same kind of reason are a candidate
standing exception, so the auditor fits {{owner}}'s judgment instead of being overruled each
time: propose the line for `### Standing exceptions` in `docs/discipline/question-ties.md`
(what kind of task, and what the auditor should accept for it). Always ASK.
{{/if}}

## 3. Place each cluster on the ladder

Search for an existing rule first: `grep -rn "<keyword>" AGENTS.md {{#if harness.coordinator_file}}{{harness.coordinator_file}} {{/if}}docs/ notes/ hooks/`
and the `REFLECT` lines of the journal (was this proposed and declined before?).

- **No rule yet** → a prose fix (a sentence in a discipline doc or a skill) is usually
  right.
- **Recurred despite a rule** → propose a mechanism (a nudge hook, a scan, a template
  field), not the same sentence again. Keeping it prose is allowed if you say why.
- **A nudge exists and is ignored, and the failure is costly** → consider a hard check
  (always ASK).

## 4. Draft fixes (at most four) and route each one

Draft at most four fixes, one per cluster that warrants one; a pass that finds nothing worth
changing says so.

For each fix write: the friction it answers (with the row count), the change, its
**narrowest scope**, and its **anti-scope** (what it must not gate; its cost). Then route it
with `notes/reflect-autonomy-heuristics.md`:

- **AUTO** (all five AUTO conditions hold) → apply it now. A hook change gets its four-branch
  selftest (`docs/discipline/hook-authoring.md`); run `bin/selftest` and proceed only on exit 0.
  Commit just that fix:

  ```bash
  git add -- <the fix's files>
  git commit -m 'chore(reflect auto): <what>' -- <the fix's files>
  bin/journal --add journal "[auto-applied] <what> — <why> (rule: AUTO-n; undo: git revert <sha>)" --kind REFLECT
  ```

- **ASK** (any ASK condition holds, or it is a boundary case) → do not apply. Queue it:

  ```bash
  bin/journal --propose "<the change in plain words — why — what it costs>" --source reflect
  ```

  It prints the proposal's id (`P…`) and writes the journal line. Then put every pending
  proposal (`bin/journal --pending`) to {{owner}} with the tradeoff and your recommendation,
{{#if harness.cap.ask_tool}}
  with the structured ask tool when there are several to accept or decline,
{{else}}
  numbered, so {{owner}} can answer "yes to 1 and 3",
{{/if}}
  and run `bin/journal --shown`. On a yes: apply, test and commit in the same turn
  (`chore(reflect): <what>`), then `bin/journal --decide <id> yes`. On a no:
  `bin/journal --decide <id> no --reason "<their reason>"`. A "continue" decides nothing:
  the proposal stays pending, and a reminder at the end of a later turn brings it back.
  Never record a proposal only in a notes file: the queue is how it reaches {{owner}}.

Before sending, reread the proposals as {{owner}} will: someone who knows the project but
did not see this session. Replace every reference to this session's events, shorthand or
run names with plain words.

## 5. Audit AUTO routing

For each auto-applied fix since the last pass: did {{owner}} revert, narrow or object to
it? Add a row to the tables in `notes/reflect-autonomy-heuristics.md` either way. An
objection of class A (one incident made into a rule) or C (a permission widened) means the
AUTO clause is too loose — propose tightening it (ASK).

## 6. Close the pass

```bash
bin/journal --add journal "pass: <n> rows read; <clusters>; <k> auto-applied, <m> proposed" --kind REFLECT
bin/journal --mark-reviewed
```

`--mark-reviewed` marks only what step 1 printed and inserts the `<!-- reviewed -->`
marker into the journal at that point. Commit the journal and the heuristics file
(`chore(reflect): pass <date>`). Report to {{owner}}: what was applied (with how to undo
it), the pending proposals as a numbered list (step 4), and what was left alone and why.
